1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,900
Not Targets but Hostages

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:32,912
When the sirens went off, Sarah Callahan had been in Bahrain thirty hours. Concrete walls, fluorescent lights, folding chairs. Beside her sat a uniform with the Korean flag on the shoulder. “Lt. Kim Junho, South Korean Navy.” A dull thud in the distance. Dust drifted down from the ceiling. His hand curled once on his knee and opened again. Sarah saw it, and pretended she hadn’t.

3
00:00:33,512 --> 00:01:03,508
“What does a liaison officer do?” “I count ships. Korean ships and Korean sailors stuck in the Gulf. How many days of water and food they have left.” A captain had told him yesterday: Lieutenant, we’re not targets, we’re more like hostages. “Aren’t you scared?” “I am. But when I start calling out the ships’ names in the morning, I’m all right. If you have something to count, you hold on.” Sarah memorized it.

4
00:01:04,108 --> 00:01:31,224
At 11:40 p.m. on March 10, Lt. Kim Junho was writing to his father. His father was a quiet man. So was the son. Between two quiet men, writing came easier than talk. Dear Father. I have not fired a shot yet. For someone like me this war is screens and numbers. But there are people behind the screens. I try not to forget that the numbers are people.

5
00:01:31,824 --> 00:01:56,708
The ships whose names I know are out on that water. The captains ask when they can leave. I didn’t know “I don’t know” could be so hard to say. Every day I learn again what you told me the day I was commissioned: lose the power to think, and you’re no longer a soldier. He wrote it by hand. The tremor of the letters, the smudge of the ink. War is lived in letters in envelopes.

6
00:01:57,308 --> 00:02:01,048
Epic Fury continues in Episode 5.
